  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1: Chances for strong storms and flash flooding\nSunday into Monday.\n\nSome stronger storms earlier this afternoon in a weak shear but\nhigher CAPE environment brought some isolated trees down from\nTazewell and Smyth down into the NC foothills/piedmont.\n\nLatest radar trends continue to show weakening showers over our\narea. There is some upstream stronger storms over eastern KY,\nbut the near term environment shows a lacking support for strong\nconvection over our area, with better instability west of the\nmountains.\n\nSunday looks to be a bit greater chance of severe development \nthan today as bulk shear looks to increase along with a possible\nshortwave traversing the upper flow and a progged increase in \ninstability. We are outlooked with a Slight Risk mainly across \nthe northern CWA with a Marginal Risk most elsewhere. The \nthreats should remain focused on strong to damaging downburst \nwinds and heavy rain/localized flash flooding. Another shortwave\nmay allow for some enhanced development of convection overnight\nSunday as well.\n\nAnother upper disturbance looks to push across the area Monday along \nwith an attendance frontal boundary that will again allow for \ndaytime showers and storms. The stronger shear and instability may \nbe more confined to the southern CWA and SPC keeps the Marginal Risk \nto our southern area as well. Behind this front we will at least see \na pattern shift with a drier airmass and more zonal flow aloft which \nwill allow for a dry midweek. This is relatively shortlived however \nas moisture and potential disturbances allow for shower and storm \nchances back by Thursday and Friday.",
